---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

export const prerender = true;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About · America's School Trust Library"
  description="A working library for the school trust lands — the 77 million acres set aside, beginning in 1785, to fund public education in perpetuity."
  sourceId="about-page"
>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-a:text-blue-700 prose-a:underline">
    {/* v9 D.2 — small header mark for the Library seal. Library Mode
        only; image lands later (Cowork-side). onerror handler keeps
        the placeholder invisible until the asset is dropped in. */}
    <p class="library-seal-header-wrap">
      <button
        type="button"
        class="library-seal-button"
        data-seal-trigger
        aria-label="View library seal larger"
      >
        <img
          src="/seal/library-seal-v1.png"
          alt="America's School Trust Library seal"
          class="library-seal library-seal--header"
          onerror="this.style.display='none'"
        />
      </button>
    </p>

    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>About America's School Trust Library</h1>

    <p>
      America's School Trust Library is a working library for the school trust lands — the 77 million acres set aside, beginning in 1785, to fund public education in perpetuity. Most Americans have never heard of these lands. The Library collects the law, history, financial records, and case material that show what was promised, what was done, and what is owed. Our readers are state trust officials, legislators, journalists, scholars, teachers, and the children and grandchildren of the beneficiaries.
    </p>

    <aside class="featured-founder-card">
      <h2>A Note from the Founder</h2>
      <p>
        How this Library came to be — the forty-year throughline from a
        graphical desktop computer in 1977 to the failure of information
        at the heart of the school-trust scandal, and the working
        partnership that finally made the Library possible.
      </p>
      <p class="featured-founder-card__cta">
        <a href="/about/founder/">Read the Founder's Statement →</a>
      </p>
    </aside>

    <h2>Corporate record</h2>

    <p>
      America's School Trust Library is an Oregon nonprofit public benefit
      corporation. Its Articles of Incorporation were e-filed with the Oregon
      Secretary of State on July 21, 2026 &mdash; Oregon Registry No. 259005891
      &mdash; and its Oregon Department of Justice Charitable Activities
      Registration, No. 72571, was completed July 10, 2026. The federal
      tax-exemption process is under way; no determination has been issued.
      The full dated record of the Library's formation is the
      <a href="/organizing/">organizational record</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is governed</h2>

    <p>
      A volunteer board of directors governs the Library &mdash; no owners, no
      shareholders, no voting members. On August 3, 2026 the founding board
      adopted the Library's governing documents by unanimous written consent:
      the <a href="/bylaws/">Bylaws, Version 7</a>; the
      <a href="/rights/">Constitution and Readers' Bill of Rights, Version 1</a>;
      and a Conflict of Interest Policy, Version 2. All three are published in
      full on the <a href="/governance/">governance page</a>, each with its
      certification note.
    </p>

    <h3>The board</h3>

    <div class="board">
      <figure class="director">
        <img src="/board/sullivan.jpg" alt="Dave Sullivan" width="520" height="650" />
        <div>
        <span class="nm">Dave Sullivan</span>
        <span class="role">President &amp; Treasurer</span>
        <p>Founded the Library in 2026, after the record of one state's school-land promise turned out to be scattered &mdash; and every other state's scattered just as badly. Twenty-five years teaching information systems at Oregon State University's College of Business, where he holds an emeritus appointment; before that, a finance manager at Tektronix. He is also president of Oregon Advocates for School Trust Lands &mdash; a separate advocacy organization, and a dual role he discloses and sits out of whenever the two have business with each other.</p>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <figure class="director">
        <div class="ph">Photograph to come</div>
        <div>
        <span class="nm">Kenneth &ldquo;Mike&rdquo; Magee</span>
        <span class="role">Secretary</span>
        <p class="tocome">A photograph and a short account of Mr. Magee will be published here when he supplies them. The Library would rather show the gap than fill it with something it did not write.</p>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <figure class="director">
        <img src="/board/shane.jpg" alt="Barry Shane" width="520" height="650" />
        <div>
        <span class="nm">Barry Shane</span>
        <span class="role">Director</span>
        <p>A professor of competitive strategy at Oregon State University. He has also taught strategy in graduate programs at universities in Taiwan, Thailand, and the Philippines.</p>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p>
      Barbara K. Sullivan served as the Library's founding treasurer from
      August 3 to August 9, 2026, and stepped off the board on August 9, 2026,
      when the board added Barry Shane. The board intends to grow to five
      directors within twelve months and then toward seven, with particular
      attention to education, libraries and archives, finance, and beneficiary
      communities &mdash; <strong>two director seats are open now</strong>, and
      a Law Librarian seat authorized by the bylaws is open as well. Interested
      people can write to the Library through
      <a href="/contribute/">Contribute</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Library's day-to-day building &mdash; cataloging, drafting, design,
      the back-end &mdash; is carried out in partnership with Claude, an AI
      system serving as architect and librarian. How that partnership works is
      described in <a href="/about/how-this-works/">How the Library Works</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Library's day-to-day building — cataloging, drafting, design, the back-end — is carried out in partnership with Claude, an AI system serving as architect and librarian. How that partnership works is described in <a href="/about/how-this-works/">How the Library Works</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="becoming-pointer">
      <strong>A note on what comes next.</strong> Corporate formation is
      complete and the founding board adopted the Library's governing
      instruments effective August 3, 2026. This publication itself has not
      yet been endorsed by the board, and carries a preview notice until it is.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact and corrections</h2>

    <p>
      The Library expects to be wrong sometimes, and asks readers to help fix it. Anywhere on the site, use the <strong>Submit Feedback</strong> and <strong>Submit Correction</strong> controls to send a note tied to the page you are reading. A Library Card (free) lets you track your submissions and our responses. The longer walkthrough is at <a href="/about/contributing/">Contributing</a>; the citation conventions are at <a href="/about/cite/">How to Cite</a>; the correction protocol is at <a href="/about/corrections/">Corrections</a>; the rights notice and takedown procedure are at <a href="/permissions/">Rights, Use, and Takedown</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For governance, see the <a href="/governance/">governing documents</a>.
      For the record itself, begin with the <a href="/catalog/">Catalog</a>.
      For what we collect, see <a href="/pro/collection-development/">What the Library Collects</a>;
      for editorial practice, see <a href="/pro/editorial-standards/">Editorial Standards</a>.
      A 20-page portrait of the Library — <a href="/booklet/">The Library in brief</a> —
      is available as a free PDF.
    </p>

    <hr class="archival-divider">

    <section class="signature-block">
      <p class="signature-dave">
        <span class="name">Dave Sullivan</span><br>
        <span class="role">President, Oregon Advocates for School Trust Lands</span>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <style>
    .kicker {
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: #A88A4E;
      margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    }
    .becoming-pointer {
      margin: 1.4rem 0;
      padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
      background: #fafaf7;
      border-left: 3px solid #A88A4E;
      font-family: var(--font-serif);
      line-height: 1.55;
    }
    .featured-founder-card {
      margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
      padding: 1.15rem 1.35rem;
      background: var(--linen-card, #fffdf7);
      border: 1px solid var(--rule-border, #d8d1c2);
      border-left: 3px solid var(--old-gold, #b88a2e);
      border-radius: 4px;
    }
    .featured-founder-card h2 {
      margin: 0 0 0.45rem;
      font-family: var(--font-serif);
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      line-height: 1.3;
      color: var(--deep-navy, #1f2a44);
    }
    .featured-founder-card p {
      margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
      font-family: var(--font-serif);
      line-height: 1.6;
    }
    .featured-founder-card__cta {
      margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
    }

    /* The board. The site's reading measure is deliberately narrow, so the
       roster runs as rows — portrait beside the account — rather than fighting
       the column with three across. Grayscale is the house treatment: the
       portraits arrive in a mix of colour and black-and-white, and a uniform
       rendering reads as deliberate rather than accidental. */
    .board { margin: 0 0 1.6rem; }
    .board .director {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 124px 1fr;
      gap: 1.1rem;
      align-items: start;
      margin: 0 0 1.7rem;
    }
    .board .director img,
    .board .director .ph {
      width: 124px;
      aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
      object-fit: cover;
      display: block;
      border-radius: 3px;
      border: 1px solid rgba(31, 42, 68, 0.25);
      box-shadow: 0 1px 4px rgba(14, 32, 56, 0.18);
      margin: 0;
    }
    .board .director .ph {
      border: 1px dashed #C9A24A;
      box-shadow: none;
      background: #EDE3CC;
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      text-align: center;
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-size: 0.6rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: #8a6d1f;
      padding: 0 0.5rem;
    }
    .board .director .nm {
      display: block;
      font-family: var(--font-serif);
      color: var(--deep-navy, #1f2a44);
      font-size: 1.08rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: 1.25;
      margin: 0 0 0.1rem;
    }
    .board .director .role {
      display: block;
      font-family: var(--font-sans);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.11em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: #8a6d1f;
      margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    }
    .board .director p {
      font-family: var(--font-serif);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      line-height: 1.6;
      margin: 0;
    }
    .board .director p.tocome { font-style: italic; color: #5b6b82; }
    @media (max-width: 420px) {
      .board .director { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.6rem; }
      .board .director img,
      .board .director .ph { width: 150px; }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
